#!/usr/bin/env python3
# emacs: -*- mode: python; py-indent-offset: 4; indent-tabs-mode: nil -*-
# vi: set ft=python sts=4 ts=4 sw=4 et:
"""Convert HTML slides into editable layered SVGs plus svgtune instructions.

Input is a deck in the file layout of Claude's "Slides" artifacts::

    <deck>/deck.json            {"order": ["<slide-id>", ...], ...}
    <deck>/slides/<slide-id>.html   one 1920x1080 <section> per slide

where elements are absolutely positioned boxes, text, inline <svg> and
<x-connector x1= y1= x2= y2= route= head=> arrows, and build steps
("clicks") are marked with ``data-build-in="fade N"`` and
``data-build-out="fade N"`` on the slide's top-level elements.

Each slide is laid out by headless Chromium (Playwright) and serialized into
plain SVG: boxes become <rect>/<path>, text stays <text> (so it remains
editable, e.g. in Inkscape), links become <a>, connectors and inline <svg>
stay vector.  Top-level elements are grouped into Inkscape layers, one per
build step, and a ``<slide-id>.svgtune`` is written which recreates every
step with svgtune::

    %file <slide-id>.svg
    layers style=display:none
    layer label:re=^base$ style=display:inline
    %save base
    layer label:re=^hed$ style=display:inline
    %save hed
    ...

Step names
  come from element ids: an element with ``id="hed--chip"`` names its build
  step "hed".  One such element per step is enough, the others with the same
  build order inherit the name.  Elements without build attributes go to
  "base".  A step without any named element falls back to "build-<N>" (an
  index, which changes when steps are inserted, so better name them).  Saved
  files are named after the steps, so inserting or reordering steps does not
  rename existing outputs.  Elements of a step which leave earlier than the
  rest of it (``data-build-out``) get their own layer "<step>~<step-left-at>".

Paint order is preserved: consecutive elements of the same step form one
layer, and elements are moved next to earlier ones of their step only when
they do not overlap anything they would jump over.

Requirements: Python 3, Playwright for Python with Chromium
(``pip install playwright && playwright install chromium``), and the fonts
used by the deck installed system-wide (for viewing/editing the SVGs as well),
or given via --fonts, or loaded from the deck's own web fonts with --web-fonts.

See https://github.com/yarikoptic/svgtune
"""
